<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gliwice</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gleiwitz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gliwice is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Founded as a town in the 13th century, it belonged to Silesian Piast duchies and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and from 1742 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, growing into an industrial centre. The staged Gleiwitz incident at its radio station on 31 August 1939 was used by Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> as a pretext for invading Poland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gliwice.jpg" alt="Gliwice" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ebielmaj · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ratusz_gliwicki_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gliwice lies in the Silesian Highlands on the Kłodnica river, a tributary of the Oder, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a>. Its name comes from a Slavic root, gliw or gliv, suggesting loamy or wet ground. The town stood on a trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, and the Gliwice Canal now links its harbour to the Oder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Gliwice was first mentioned as a town in 1276, though Duke Władysław Opolski of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a> had granted it town rights earlier. It belonged to several Piast duchies and was the capital of its own Duchy of Gliwice from 1322 to 1342. In 1335 it came under the suzerainty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Bohemian Crown</a>, which passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1526, and in 1532 it was incorporated into the Habsburg monarchy as Gleiwitz. In the Silesian Wars of the mid-18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> took it. The first coke-fired blast furnace on the European continent was built there in 1796 under John Baildon, and industry turned it into a major city.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> a plebiscite on 20 March 1921 gave Gleiwitz 32,029 votes for remaining in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and 8,558 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> left it in Germany, near the new Polish border. On 31 August 1939 German secret police staged the Gleiwitz incident, a false-flag attack on the radio station, which Nazi Germany used as a pretext to invade Poland. During the war the Germans ran prisoner-of-war and forced-labour camps in the city, including four subcamps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> from July 1944. Its New Synagogue had been destroyed in 1938.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> occupied the city on 24 January 1945, and Soviet troops murdered over 1,000 civilians. Gliwice was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on 18 March 1945, after almost three centuries outside Polish rule. The German population was expelled and replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, many of them displaced from the east, among them academics from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a> who founded the Silesian University of Technology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gliwice is a city of 175,102 permanent residents in 2021 and an industrial and university centre that moved from steelworks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining towards automotive and machine industries after the 1990s. Its Old Town keeps medieval walls and churches, Piast Castle and the Market Square. The Radio Tower, 118 metres high, is the last remaining wooden radio tower in the world and is a Historic Monument of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gliwice” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>SS operatives seized the German radio station at <b>Gleiwitz</b> and broadcast an anti-German message, posing as Polish raiders.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-31">31 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Casimir received Bytom, an old market town in eastern Upper Silesia, together with lands around <b>Gliwice</b> and Koźle.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bytom">Duchy of Bytom</a></li></ul></section>
